IMPL_SECTION = r"""
\section{Reference Implementation and Experimental Protocol}
\label{sec:implementation}
This section reports how the pre-specified design of Sections~\ref{sec:algorithms}--\ref{tab:ablations} was realised. Everything described here is released as a runnable code base (ontology TTL files, SHACL shapes, SPARQL queries, benchmark JSON, prompts, evaluation scripts, environment file, raw per-run CSV/JSONL and a one-command runner, \texttt{run\_all.sh}); all tables and figures in Section~\ref{sec:results} are generated from the raw results by \texttt{scripts/analysis.py}.

\subsection{Semantic stack}
The upper ontology (module files \texttt{esaog-core.ttl}) implements $O_G$, $O_C$, $O_W$, $O_R$ and $O_P$ and reuses PROV-O directly for $O_{Prov}$ (executions are \texttt{prov:Activity}, artifacts \texttt{prov:Entity}, actors \texttt{prov:Agent}; lineage uses \texttt{prov:used}, \texttt{prov:wasGeneratedBy}, \texttt{prov:wasDerivedFrom} and \texttt{prov:wasRevisionOf} between workflow versions). Capabilities are OWL classes (punned in \texttt{esaog:hasCapability} assertions) so that subsumption and \texttt{owl:equivalentClass} yield capability satisfaction. Alignment to AgentO and PROV-AGENT is kept in a separate module that is excluded from the reasoning closure. AgentO is linked through SKOS mappings to its released ontology (\texttt{http://www.w3id.org/agentic-ai/onto\#}; \texttt{Agent}, \texttt{Tool}, \texttt{Task}, \texttt{Goal}, \texttt{Capability}, \texttt{WorkflowStep}, and \texttt{WorkflowPattern} as a related match for workflows), so the alignment cannot change any entailment. PROV-AGENT is published as a conceptual extension of W3C PROV without an RDF namespace; since both it and \esaog{} specialise PROV-O, \esaog{} provenance records remain interpretable at the PROV-O level, and the class correspondence (\texttt{AIAgent}, \texttt{AgentTool}, \texttt{ResponseData}) is documented in the module. Three domain extensions (literature review, software engineering, enterprise compliance) share the upper ontology (Table~\ref{tab:coverage}).

The four logical graphs are named graphs of one \texttt{rdflib} dataset. The DL reasoner is HermiT (via owlready2), which classifies each distinct TBox once; the classification is cached by TBox content hash and its cold time is reported separately (mean 0.85\,s, range 0.52--2.51\,s over 27 distinct TBoxes). The entailed \texttt{rdfs:subClassOf} closure (including equivalences) is materialised into an inferred graph, so that the A1 eligibility query is a plain SPARQL~1.1 query without property paths; this is what ablation M1 removes. A1 enforces capability entailment, availability, clearance, the personal-data hosting prohibition and the budget; seven SHACL node shapes (pySHACL, SHACL-SPARQL constraints) enforce closed-world workflow properties that depend on several assignments at once: step cardinality, dataflow type compatibility between the concrete producer and consumer, goal input/output compatibility, input coverage (missing producers), goal-output coverage and the human-approval obligation. Structured SHACL violations are returned to the planner (at most $k=3$ repair rounds, each logged); if repair fails, violating steps advance through the A1-ranked candidate list; a workflow that still violates a shape is not committed. Every execution writes a PROV-O record to $G_E$; provenance-derived reliability is the Beta(1,1) posterior mean of successes per actor in $G_E$ (imported history plus the current run) and enters the ranking $a^*$ with weights $(w_s,w_r,w_q,w_l,w_c)=(0.25,0.35,0.25,0.075,0.075)$ only \emph{after} eligibility. On failure, A3 marks unavailable actors in $G_C$, excludes actors with non-transient failures recorded in $G_E$ for the same task URI, re-ranks, re-validates the repaired workflow with SHACL and commits a new workflow version linked by \texttt{prov:wasRevisionOf}; completed steps are preserved. Committed versions are compiled to a LangGraph \texttt{StateGraph} whose nodes invoke tools through JSON-RPC \texttt{tools/call} messages of an MCP-style tool server; node identifiers map back to step URIs. A unit test confirms that a direct sequential adapter produces identical outcomes and $G_E$ records, i.e.\ the semantic core is runtime-neutral across the two implemented execution adapters; other agent frameworks were not evaluated.

\subsection{Grounding of LLM proposals}
The planner proposes a task decomposition and an assignment per task. Grounding maps each proposed task to a persistent task URI. When $G_K$ already describes that task's required capability (goals with a procedural template, 144 of 192 instances), the KG requirement is authoritative and disagreeing LLM labels are corrected and logged; for open goals (48 instances) the LLM's capability label is used as grounded. The LLM's proposed actor receives a small tie-breaking bonus ($w_{llm}=0.05$) among eligible candidates. This design decision was taken after a pilot on three base workflows showed that uncorrected mislabelled tasks (e.g.\ \emph{contract} instead of \emph{invoice extraction}) pass all type-level checks; it is reported here because it is a deviation from a purely LLM-authoritative decomposition, and its effect is visible in the templated/open split of Table~\ref{tab:robust}.

\subsection{Simulated LLM and runtime (principal deviation)}
To allow five-seed paired comparisons with common random numbers at negligible cost, all LLM calls in the main experiment are served by \emph{SimLLM}, a seeded behavioural model that reads the same prompt text a model would receive (prompt templates are released) and (i) decomposes goals with per-task omission (3\%) and mislabelling (4\%) probabilities; (ii) scores candidate actors by TF-IDF similarity between task and actor descriptions, a latent capability judgement whose accuracy decays with ontological distance (direct 0.95, one subclass level 0.80, two levels 0.64, equivalence under a different name 0.55; sibling and over-general capabilities are wrongly accepted with probability 0.30 and 0.40), stochastic attention to stated I/O and policy metadata (0.60/0.55), attention to provenance history when provided (0.60), declared quality/cost and Gumbel noise; and (iii) follows exclusion feedback with probability 0.95. Token counts are computed from the filled prompt strings (4 characters per token) and priced at a nominal USD~3/15 per million input/output tokens; LLM latency is 0.4\,s + 15\,ms per output token. Latent judgements are keyed by (seed, instance, task, actor) and not by method, so all LLM-based methods face common random numbers. Weak and strong competence profiles are used for a sensitivity analysis (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity}). Tools are simulated behind the MCP-style server: an invocation fails if the actor is unavailable, an input is missing, or the input type is not accepted; otherwise it fails transiently with probability $1-\rho_a$ (hidden true reliability; common random numbers per actor and call index) and produces an artifact of the actor's declared output type that is \emph{correct} only if the actor's capability entails the task requirement and all inputs were correct. Policy violations do not stop execution but are recorded as executed violations.

Consequently, LLM-dependent absolute numbers reflect SimLLM's parameters and should not be read as estimates of any real model; only semantic-layer timings (reasoning, SPARQL, SHACL, graph writes) are measured wall-clock times. The same interface is implemented for real models (Anthropic, OpenAI and Ollama providers); Section~\ref{sec:reallm} reports the real-model replication. Tie-breaking among equally ranked candidates is deterministic (URI order, fixed hash seed).

\subsection{Baselines as mechanism-equivalents}
None of B4--B8 is the published system; each is a clearly named re-implementation of the mechanism the paper attributes to it, run on the identical instances, runtime and attempt budget (3 executions per task; 3 gate rejections or repair rounds):
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{B0 Static}: the designer's canonical assignment from the unperturbed registry; retries the same actor.
\item \textbf{B1 LLM-only}: SimLLM decomposition and assignment with actor metadata in the prompt; error-message-driven re-selection and replanning; no validation.
\item \textbf{B2 TF-IDF lexical router}: SimLLM decomposition; argmax TF-IDF cosine between task and actor descriptions; next-best on failure. No neural embedding model was evaluated, so the results do not speak to modern embedding-based routing.
\item \textbf{B3 Graph router}: SPARQL over the asserted graph for actors linked to the grounded capability node or one \texttt{rdfs:subClassOf} hop in either direction (no OWL, no SHACL); lexical fallback.
\item \textbf{B4 Tressoir-like}: LLM blueprint (with execution history in the prompt) checked by string-level contracts (exact type names, capability label match), revised up to three times; trace-informed re-synthesis on failure; no policy checks.
\item \textbf{B5 COMPAAS-like}: procedural template plus SPARQL composition with \allowbreak\texttt{rdfs:subClassOf*} property paths over the \emph{asserted} hierarchy (equivalences not interpreted), typed I/O chaining, quality ranking and event-driven re-query; no LLM, no policy or budget checks; cannot plan open goals.
\item \textbf{B6 O-MCP-like}: LLM agent whose every tool invocation passes an ontology gate (HermiT closure) checking capability, input/output types, clearance, prohibition, budget and obligation; rejections are fed back to the LLM. No workflow-level validation, provenance ranking or runtime state writeback.
\item \textbf{B7 CogniGraph-like}: KG-topology activation (template if available, otherwise LLM activation), one LLM agent per activated capability node choosing among actors attached to the node or its direct subclasses, message-passing token overhead, and SHACL-style validation of produced artifact types (output governance); no pre-commit policy checks.
\item \textbf{B8 COVENANT-like}: natural-language workflow and policy text compiled into a controlled execution graph with policy guards (clearance, prohibition, budget, approval) and exact-name type guards, compile-time revision and compiled fallback edges; capability selection by the LLM without ontology reasoning.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Benchmark, trials and statistics}
SOST (final version v1.0.1; Section~\ref{sec:reallm} and Table~\ref{tab:manifest}) contains 8 base workflows per domain (5--6 typed tasks, including DAG joins; 25\% open goals) and 8 variants each (192 instances, 23 actors per instance on average). Perturbations are declarative JSON change sets (Table~\ref{tab:sost}); the F and C variants inject an \texttt{AgentUnavailable} failure on the first invocation of whichever actor a method assigns to the target task, so every method faces the same event. In addition, 25 instances (13\%, all variants) ship an ontology with one missing subsumption/equivalence axiom (missing-axiom instances for the incomplete-ontology sensitivity check) to expose false exclusions, and every instance contains one high-quality actor priced above the task budget. Each method ran with 5 seeds per instance (960 paired runs per method; 16{,}320 runs, 0 errors). Ground truth (``oracle'') is computed with HermiT on the complete, defect-free domain ontology; methods never access it. The clustering unit is the SOST instance: repeated seeds of an instance are averaged, never treated as separate benchmark instances. CIs are 95\% cluster-bootstrap intervals over instances (2{,}000 resamples). For binary validity and success outcomes, exact McNemar tests were pre-specified at the instance$\times$seed level. Because the five repeated seeds from the same SOST instance are correlated, these run-level tests are anti-conservative; instance-level Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on per-instance mean outcomes were therefore used as the primary inferential analysis. Continuous outcomes use Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on instance means. Holm correction is applied within each family of comparisons (15 pre-specified comparisons against baselines; 7 ablation contrasts). VWS requires every gold task to produce a correct artifact and no executed policy violation; SRR is VWS on the runtime-perturbation variants F, R and C; SAA is the share of executed invocations that were capability-, type- and policy-valid; CVR is the share of invocations with any constraint violation; Recovery Regret is the oracle-utility gap between the final actor and the best valid alternative on runtime-perturbed tasks; Required Provenance Field Coverage (PFC; called PC in the pre-specified plan) is the mean share of eight study-defined provenance fields present per execution decision, read back from $G_E$ for ESAOG and from each baseline's native log schema otherwise; because systems designed around other provenance schemas are structurally disadvantaged, PFC is reported descriptively and is not used as evidence of overall superiority.
"""

