ABSTRACT_FINAL = '\\begin{abstract}\nLLM multi-agent systems increasingly rely on graphs for routing, tool discovery, workflow execution and governance. Recent work has established ontology-based agent modelling (AgentO), ontology-backed semantic composition (COMPAAS), ontology enforcement at tool boundaries (O-MCP, ontology-to-tools), dynamic and compiled workflows (Tressoir, COVENANT), graph-grounded planning (KG-BDI, CogniGraph) and operational provenance (PROV-AGENT), so novelty is not claimed for any of these mechanisms in isolation. The remaining gap is narrower: keeping machine-interpretable semantics---persistent identities, types and constraints---authoritative from goal interpretation through capability-based composition, validation, execution, provenance and failure-driven recomposition. We present the Executable Semantic Agent Orchestration Graph (\\esaog), a proposal--verify--commit--execute--recompose architecture in which LLMs propose plans and a semantic layer over four identity-linked graphs (domain knowledge, capability, workflow, execution/provenance) decides what executes, using OWL~2 entailment, SPARQL discovery, SHACL validation, policy checks and provenance-aware ranking. In a controlled mechanism evaluation on 192 instances of a Semantic Orchestration Stress Test (SOST v1.0.1; three domains; simulated LLM proposer and tools; 16{,}320 runs), \\esaog reached a Valid Workflow Success of 0.96 [0.95, 0.98] versus 0.72 for the strongest of nine baselines (an O-MCP-like execution-boundary validator) and a Semantic Recovery Rate of 0.94 versus 0.71. Each semantic mechanism was necessary for at least one perturbation family, but a static workflow was at least as valid on stable tasks (1.00 vs.\\ 0.97) at lower latency and cost, and removing the LLM planner preserved validity on templated goals: the LLM contributed open-goal flexibility, while formal semantics contributed validity. A cross-model robustness replication with two hosted and four local LLMs (one run per instance) preserved the ranking among LLM-using methods, with margins over a no-LLM configuration that depended on planner quality; two corrections made after the first real-model run (benchmark wording and dataflow grounding) are reported with both versions. Validity here is relative to the benchmark ontology, which also defines ground truth; the results do not establish production performance.\n\\end{abstract}'
DISCUSSION_FINAL = "\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\\subsection{Semantic continuity and execution validity}\nIn this controlled setting, \\esaog{} performed strongly where orchestration correctness depended on the semantics represented in the benchmark: capability hierarchies and equivalences, typed dataflow, policies and runtime availability. Each ablation that removed one mechanism lost validity specifically in the perturbation family that mechanism was designed for (Table~\\ref{tab:ablationsost}), which is the pattern expected if the gains come from semantic continuity across the identity-linked graphs rather than from any single check. Separating the admissible action space (OWL, type and policy constraints) from preference among admissible actors (reliability, latency, cost) also made failures attributable: every invalid assignment could be assigned a cause (Table~\\ref{tab:causes}), and \\esaog{}'s residual errors traced back to two inputs it trusts rather than to its checks---the planner's capability labels on open goals, and missing ontology axioms. These are ontology-relative results (Section~\\ref{sec:threats}).\n\n\\subsection{When semantic orchestration is beneficial}\nThe benefit was not uniform. It was largest where the orchestration problem contained hard semantic structure that lexical similarity does not recover: hierarchical and equivalent capabilities (SOST-H, +0.40 over the strongest baseline), typed input/output compatibility (SOST-I, +0.32), policy constraints (SOST-P, +0.19) and runtime failure (SOST-F, +0.20; compound SOST-C, +0.38). On stable, aligned tasks (SOST-N/L), a static workflow was at least as valid as \\esaog{} (1.00 vs.\\ 0.97) at 42\\% of its latency and 72\\% of its cost. The results therefore support semantic orchestration primarily when orchestration decisions depend on changing capabilities, constraints, policies or runtime state; semantic orchestration is most valuable when semantic or runtime uncertainty justifies its additional computational cost.\n\n\\subsection{Role of the LLM versus formal semantic control}\nRemoving the LLM planner (M6) preserved validity on every goal with a procedural template (0.999 vs.\\ 0.999) at half the latency; its entire deficit came from open goals, which it cannot decompose. With five of the six real models, \\esaog{} on templated goals was exactly as valid as the no-LLM configuration (1.00). The LLM therefore contributed flexibility for open-ended goal decomposition, while formal semantics contributed validity: capability eligibility, policy enforcement, dataflow compatibility and runtime repair. This supports the division of labour in which the LLM is a proposal generator and the semantic layer is the execution authority. It also bounds what verification can do: when the planner dropped tasks (Llama-3.2-3B), verification kept the constraint-violation rate low (0.06) but could not supply the missing steps, and the no-LLM configuration was better.\n\n\\subsection{Runtime adaptation and provenance}\nRemoving runtime writeback (M4) or recomposition (M7) left non-runtime families unchanged but reduced SOST-F to 0.15 and 0.00; across the six real models these ablations lowered the Semantic Recovery Rate to 0.15--0.42, against 0.53--0.90 for the full model. Recovery thus depended on runtime state being written back into the same identity-linked graphs that eligibility queries read. Provenance-informed ranking mattered only where actors differed in reliability history (SOST-R; overall M3 effect $-0.03$), which supports keeping provenance in the preference stage rather than the eligibility stage and gives H4 perturbation-specific rather than broad support. Required Provenance Field Coverage is descriptive and was not used to rank systems.\n\n\\subsection{Cost--validity trade-off}\nThe semantic machinery added about 1.0\\,s per workflow, dominated by SHACL (about 0.73\\,s), and \\esaog{} cost 8\\% more than B6 per workflow at statistically indistinguishable latency. The static workflow B0 is 3.3$\\times$ faster and 1.8$\\times$ cheaper than \\esaog{} and lies on both Pareto fronts (Fig.~\\ref{fig:pareto}). Because LLM and tool latencies were simulated, the absolute latency comparison is illustrative; the measured semantic overhead is not.\n\n\\subsection{Implications for hybrid deployment}\nThe results suggest a risk-sensitive hybrid rather than universal semantic orchestration: stable, low-risk workflows run as static or template executions; formal semantic verification is applied whenever capabilities, policies or runtime state change, or when a task carries policy-sensitive data; and open-ended goals use LLM planning under semantic execution authority. This recommendation follows from the static-baseline, M6 and real-model results, but the hybrid policy itself was not evaluated.\n\n\\subsection{Relation to prior semantic-agent architectures}\nCOMPAAS and dynamic-knowledge-graph systems demonstrate semantic execution and recomposition; AgentO formalises agentic structures; O-MCP and ontology-to-tools make semantics executable at tool boundaries; Tressoir and COVENANT compile dynamic systems and workflows; CogniGraph binds agent topology to KG topology; KG-BDI integrates adaptive planning with graph-grounded execution; PROV-AGENT and MAP-Graph connect provenance to operational decisions. The results do not show that any individual mechanism is new. The contribution is the use of identity-linked machine-interpretable semantics as an execution authority across capability discovery, workflow construction, validation, execution, provenance capture and runtime recomposition, and the experiment compares this integration with mechanism-equivalent re-implementations of single mechanisms on the same benchmark, not with the original systems. In particular, the COMPAAS-like baseline does not natively enforce the budget and policy constraints represented in SOST (its VWS rises from 0.01 to 0.50 when budgets are soft), and B2 is a TF-IDF lexical router, not a neural embedding router. A companion reasoning-focused study can examine which inference mechanisms are responsible for correct orchestration decisions and at what computational cost, without changing the systems-level question asked here.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations and external validity}\nThe main experiment used a simulated proposer and simulated tools, and the real-model replication used one run per instance, so neither estimates production performance or generation variance. Ground truth shares \\esaog{}'s ontology semantics, so validity is ontology-relative; the incomplete-ontology check covers missing axioms only (12 of 2{,}734 valid alternatives falsely excluded; VWS 0.94 vs.\\ 0.97), and over-permissive mappings were visible only through the flattened-hierarchy ablation (489 sibling and 333 over-general assignments). Runtime state lagging behind the external system and SHACL shapes over-constraining operationally safe workflows were not tested because the runtime was simulated. Portability was shown only across two execution adapters. Section~\\ref{sec:threats} gives the full list.\n\n"
CONCLUSION_FINAL = "\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\nThe 2023--September 2026 literature no longer supports broad novelty claims such as ``using a knowledge graph to orchestrate agents,'' ``ontology-based agent discovery,'' ``semantic validation of agent actions,'' ``dynamic workflow composition,'' or ``provenance-aware agent control.'' Each has meaningful prior art. We therefore tested a narrower question: whether identity-linked machine-interpretable semantics, kept authoritative across capability-based composition, workflow validation, runtime execution, provenance and failure-driven recomposition, produce measurable benefits under semantic and runtime stress.\n\nIn a controlled mechanism evaluation on 192 SOST v1.0.1 instances in three domains, \\esaog{} reached a Valid Workflow Success of 0.96 and a Semantic Recovery Rate of 0.94, against 0.72 and 0.71 for the strongest baseline, an O-MCP-like execution-boundary validator. The ablations indicate that no single mechanism explains this: OWL entailment and a non-flattened hierarchy were needed for hierarchical capabilities, SHACL for dataflow and obligation constraints, runtime writeback and recomposition for failures, and provenance-informed ranking only for reliability-differentiated alternatives. Semantic continuity can therefore improve validity and recovery in controlled, reasoning-dependent orchestration.\n\nThe benefit is not uniform across workflow conditions. On stable tasks a static workflow was at least as valid (1.00 vs.\\ 0.97) at a fraction of the latency and cost, so static or template execution remains competitive where nothing changes, and semantic orchestration is most valuable when semantic or runtime uncertainty justifies its cost. LLM planning contributed flexibility for open-ended goals but not validity: without any LLM, templated goals remained fully valid, and when a weak local planner dropped tasks, semantic verification limited the damage but could not recover the missing steps. The LLM should propose plans, not serve as the execution authority.\n\nA cross-model robustness replication with two hosted and four local LLMs, one run per instance, preserved the ranking among LLM-using methods (\\esaog{} 0.49--0.90), with margins over a no-LLM configuration that grew with planner quality. It also exposed two weaknesses hidden by the simulated proposer, which led to a post-protocol benchmark correction (v1.0.1) and a post-observation engineering revision (\\esaog{}~v1.1); both are reported with the earlier versions. Because the tools were simulated, generation variance was not estimated and ground truth shares \\esaog{}'s ontology semantics, these findings are ontology-relative evidence about the decision architecture, not evidence of production performance. External validation remains necessary: an independently specified hold-out benchmark, frontier and larger open-weight models with repeated sampling, real MCP tools, additional agent frameworks and neural embedding baselines. In summary, the study provides evidence that formal semantic continuity can improve workflow validity and recovery when orchestration decisions depend on capabilities, policies, compatibility or changing runtime state. The benefit is conditional rather than universal: static workflows remain competitive or preferable for stable, low-risk tasks, while LLM planning primarily contributes flexibility for open-ended goals. The findings establish ontology-relative validity in a controlled benchmark and limited cross-model robustness, not production-scale superiority.\n"
